<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · 1739 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Hark! The Herald Angels Sing</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Christmas hymn written by Charles Wesley in 1739. Its famous tune was added in 1855 from music by Mendelssohn.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Charles Wesley; revised by George Whitefield and others</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Felix Mendelssohn (melody from Festgesang, 1840), adapted by William Hayman Cummings (1855)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the early Methodists of England; later Anglican and other English-speaking churches</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1739; present melody fitted in 1855</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics by Charles Wesley (1739), revised by George Whitefield (1753), Martin Madan (1760) and Hymns Ancient and Modern (1861); melody by Felix Mendelssohn (1840), adapted 1855)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hark-the-herald-angels-sing">Hark! The Herald Angels Sing</a>&quot; is an English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">Christmas carol</a> written by Charles Wesley in 1739. It is based on Luke 2:8–14 and describes a chorus of angels singing of the birth of Christ. Wesley called it &quot;Hymn for Christmas-Day&quot;, and it first appeared in Hymns and Sacred Poems (1739), compiled by Charles and his brother John. His version had ten four-line verses and a different opening, in which the &quot;welkin&quot;, the sky, rings with glory to the King of Kings.</p><p>The text sung today is the work of several hands. George Whitefield, like the Wesleys an early leader of the Methodist movement, included the hymn in his Collection of Hymns for Social Worship (1753); he left out the eighth and tenth verses and revised the rest, above all the opening couplet, which he changed to the line about the herald angels that gives the carol its name. Whitefield's version was taken into Martin Madan's Collection of Psalms and Hymns (1760) with a new ending to the second verse. The carol first appeared in an Anglican publication in 1782, in a supplement to Tate and Brady's New Version of the Psalms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>. Because the New Version and its supplement were bound up with every copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a>, the carol spread widely and became one of the &quot;great four&quot; Anglican hymns. A last change, to the end of the fourth verse, was made when it entered Hymns Ancient and Modern in 1861. The version most often sung now uses six of Wesley's verses, arranged as three eight-line verses with a refrain.</p><p>The tune came later still. Wesley had meant the words to be sung to the same tune as his Easter hymn &quot;Christ the Lord Is Risen Today&quot;. In 1855 the British musician William Hayman Cummings, organist at Waltham Abbey Church, adapted a movement from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-mendelssohn">Felix Mendelssohn</a>'s Festgesang zum Gutenbergfest (1840), a secular work composed for the 400th anniversary of the invention of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press, to fit the words. Since the middle of the 19th century the carol has usually been sung to this melody.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> it is commonly heard in an arrangement that keeps Cummings's harmonisation for the first two verses and adds, in the third, a soprano descant and a new organ harmonisation by Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Willcocks, first published by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> University Press in 1961 in the first book of Carols for Choirs. For many years it has been the recessional hymn of the Festival of Nine Lessons and Carols at King's College Chapel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. At St Patrick's Cathedral, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, the same service traditionally ends with the hymn sung to &quot;See, the Conqu'ring hero comes&quot; from Handel's Judas Maccabaeus, with brass fanfare, drums and organ, broadcast live each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Eve on RTÉ Radio 1. The Victorian organist W. H. Jude also wrote a setting. The carol is sung by the children's choir in the closing scene of A Charlie Brown Christmas and is heard in The Muppet Christmas Carol.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of praise for the birth of Christ, based on the angels' song to the shepherds in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospel-of-luke">Gospel of Luke</a>. It calls all nations to join the angels in proclaiming the newborn king, and sets out in a few lines what Christians believe about him: God become man, born to live among people. The mood is triumphant.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hark! the herald-angels sing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Glory to the new-born King,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Peace on earth, and mercy mild,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God and sinners reconciled.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Joyful, all ye nations, rise,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Join the triumph of the skies;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With the angelic host proclaim</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Christ is born in Bethlehem.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hark! the herald-angels sing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Glory to the new-born King.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Christ, by highest heaven adored,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Christ, the Everlasting Lord,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Late in time behold Him come,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Offspring of a Virgin's womb.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Veiled in flesh the Godhead see</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hail, the Incarnate Deity!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Pleased as Man with man to dwell,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Jesus, our Emmanuel.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hark! the herald-angels sing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Glory to the new-born King.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hail, the heaven-born Prince of Peace!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hail, the Sun of Righteousness!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Light and Life to all He brings,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Risen with healing in His wings.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Mild He lays His glory by,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Born that man no more may die,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Born to raise the sons of earth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Born to give them second birth.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hark! the herald-angels sing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Glory to the new-born King.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Charles Wesley (1739), as altered in Hymns Ancient and Modern (1861); as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from Wikipedia (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hark!_The_Herald_Angels_Sing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hark! The Herald Angels Sing</a>); public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="9Bwn0k0k8xI" data-title="Hark! The Herald Angels Sing: the Choir of King's College, Cambridge, with David Willcocks's descant (Carols from King's 2021; the choir's own channel)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">the Choir of King's College, Cambridge, with David Willcocks's descant (Carols from King's 2021; the choir's own channel)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel KingsCollegeChoir. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9Bwn0k0k8xI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Collection of Hymns for Social Worship (George Whitefield)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1753</span><span class="sg-chain-h">revised text with the present opening</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Collection of Psalms and Hymns (Martin Madan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1760</span><span class="sg-chain-h">further revised text</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hark! The Herald Angels Sing (William Hayman Cummings)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1855</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the words fitted to Mendelssohn's melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hark! The Herald Angels Sing (David Willcocks, Carols for Choirs)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1961</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement with descant</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Music and the Higher Life (W. H. Jude)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">A Charlie Brown Christmas</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung by the children's choir in the closing scene</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Muppet Christmas Carol</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in the film</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">South Park, &quot;Merry Christmas, Charlie Manson!&quot;</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody of the Charlie Brown scene</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Luke 2:8–14</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the Gospel passage on which the hymn is based</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Festgesang zum Gutenbergfest (Felix Mendelssohn)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1840</span><span class="sg-chain-h">source of the present melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">See, the Conqu'ring hero comes, from Judas Maccabaeus (Handel)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody used at St Patrick's Cathedral, Dublin</span></li></ul></section>
